# CyberCareer — environment configuration
# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in the values you want. All keys are OPTIONAL:
# without them the app still works (template cover letters, mailto email fallback).

# ── TLS (local dev only) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 ONLY if outbound HTTPS fails to validate certificates on your dev
# machine (e.g. behind a corporate/AV TLS-intercepting proxy). This disables
# certificate verification process-wide — NEVER enable it in production.
# ALLOW_INSECURE_TLS=1

# ── AI / LLM (Writer Agent: cover letters + learning roadmap) ──────────────
# Provide ONE key below. By default the first provider with a key set is used,
# in this order: Anthropic, Gemini, OpenRouter, OpenAI. To force a specific one
# regardless of which keys are present, set LLM_PROVIDER.
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini        # anthropic | gemini | openrouter | openai

# Anthropic Claude (paid)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6

# Google Gemini — FREE tier, no credit card. Key: https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# OpenRouter — FREE models (suffix ":free"). Key: https://openrouter.ai/keys
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-chat-v3-0324
#
# Careful with ":free" slugs. Probed 2026-07-30 on a real account: deepseek-r1:free,
# llama-3.3-70b-instruct:free, qwen3-coder:free, gemma-4-31b-it:free and
# deepseek-chat-v3-0324:free ALL answer HTTP 404 "unavailable for free". OpenRouter
# withdraws free tiers regularly, so check a slug before relying on it. The model
# above works but BILLS PER TOKEN — leave this unset to stay free.

# OpenAI (paid)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o

# ── RAG embeddings (semantic job matching + grounded CareerBot) ────────────
# Reuses the GEMINI_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY above. Gemini is preferred (free),
# then OpenAI. Set EMBED_PROVIDER to force one. Without a key, the app falls back
# to keyword matching and canned chat answers (no error).
# EMBED_PROVIDER=gemini        # gemini | openai
# GEMINI_EMBED_MODEL=gemini-embedding-001
# OPENAI_EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# ── Email notifications (send application by email) ────────────────────────
# Uses the Resend HTTP API (https://resend.com). Without it, the app falls back
# to opening the user's mail client via a mailto: link.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_FROM=CyberCareer <onboarding@resend.dev>

# ── Optional job-search providers (already supported) ──────────────────────
# APIFY_TOKEN=
# JOOBLE_API_KEY=
# Adzuna — free aggregator API (surfaces StepStone/Indeed jobs legitimately).
# Register for free at https://developer.adzuna.com to get an app id + key.
# ADZUNA_APP_ID=
# ADZUNA_APP_KEY=

# ── Identity & accounts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public URL the browser reaches this app on. The OIDC redirect URI is derived
# from it, and must match what you register at the provider EXACTLY.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Usernames that get the admin role (comma-separated). Admins can open the Admin
# page and read /api/admin/db — every account and every saved application. This
# is env-only on purpose: a user cannot grant it to themselves.
#
# With AUTH_MODE=oidc-only the username is derived from the provider's
# preferred_username claim, so list THAT, not a local name.
# ADMIN_USERS=student

# ── Feedback digest ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where to mail a summary of new anonymous feedback. Unset means no mail is sent;
# the Feedback tab still shows everything to an admin.
#
# A DIGEST, not one mail per submission. /api/feedback is public and unauthenticated
# on purpose — that is what makes the answers worth having — so mailing on every
# submission would turn it into an amplifier: at the rate limit already in force
# (10/hour per address) one client can generate 240 messages a day, against a free
# Resend tier of 100. One summary per period is bounded whatever the volume.
#
# Needs RESEND_API_KEY set as well.
# FEEDBACK_DIGEST_TO=you@example.com
# FEEDBACK_DIGEST_HOURS=24

# ── Who may call the expensive endpoints ───────────────────────────────────
# Everything that costs money or CPU — every LLM call, PDF parsing, the paid
# scrapers — requires a signed-in session. That is not configurable: rate limiting
# bounds abuse, it does not forbid a stranger spending your budget every hour.
#
# Job search is the exception, left open so a visitor can see what the app does
# before creating an account. It is still rate-limited. Set to 0 to close it too,
# and /api/jobs, /api/scrape-all and /api/job-count will require a login as well.
# PUBLIC_JOB_SEARCH=1

# ── Who may hold a password here ───────────────────────────────────────────
# both      (default) username/password AND any configured provider.
# oidc-only identity is delegated entirely to the provider below. This app then
#           refuses /api/login, /api/register and /api/account/password, hides the
#           local forms, and sends "Sign in" / "Create an account" to the
#           provider's own pages.
#
# Pick oidc-only when the provider's admin console must be the single place where
# accounts are administered: a local password would keep working after you disable
# a user there, which defeats the point. Ignored (falls back to "both") when no
# OIDC provider is configured, so you cannot lock yourself out.
#
# Existing local accounts are NOT deleted — they simply cannot sign in with a
# password any more. They re-attach automatically when the same person signs in
# through the provider with a confirmed matching email.
# AUTH_MODE=oidc-only

# ── Sign in with an identity provider (OIDC) ───────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of provider ids to offer. Each id needs its own block
# below. Leave empty and only username/password login is shown — nothing breaks.
#
# The intended setup is ONE self-hosted provider (Keycloak, Authentik, Zitadel,
# Logto) which itself brokers Google / GitHub / LinkedIn / Microsoft. Adding a
# social network is then a change inside that provider, not in this app.
#
# OIDC_PROVIDERS=keycloak
#
# Redirect URI to register at the provider:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback
#
# Also register a post-logout redirect URI, or signing out will end at a provider
# error page instead of coming back here. In Keycloak: Clients → your client →
# "Valid post logout redirect URIs":
#   http://localhost:3000/
#
# For "Create an account" to work, the provider must allow self-registration. In
# Keycloak: Realm settings → Login → User registration → On.
#
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080/realms/careerai
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=careerai-web
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=
# LABEL is the only provider name the UI shows, and with a single provider the
# sign-in buttons drop it entirely ("Sign in", not "Sign in with X"). Keep it
# neutral — the identity server's product name means nothing to your users, and it
# is misleading once that server brokers Google/GitHub.
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_LABEL=Single sign-on
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_SCOPES=openid profile email

# ── Google / GitHub sign-in ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Do NOT add these as providers here. Configure them INSIDE Keycloak instead:
#   Keycloak admin console → your realm → Identity providers → Google / GitHub
# The Keycloak login page then shows the Google and GitHub buttons, this app keeps
# exactly one button, and no client secret for either lives in this file.
#
# Adding GitHub here would not work anyway: GitHub is plain OAuth2 with no
# /.well-known/openid-configuration, and this implementation is discovery-based.
# Keycloak has a built-in GitHub social provider that handles the difference.
#
# Redirect URI to register at Google / GitHub (the BROKER endpoint, not this app):
#   http://localhost:8080/realms/careerai/broker/google/endpoint
#   http://localhost:8080/realms/careerai/broker/github/endpoint
#
# ONLY needed when this app also runs in Docker. Inside the app container
# "localhost:8080" is the app container itself, not Keycloak, so the server needs a
# second route to the same provider. The browser keeps using ISSUER above for the
# login redirect; discovery / token / userinfo go through this one.
# OIDC_KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_ISSUER=http://keycloak:8080/realms/careerai
#
# Any OIDC provider works the same way — Google directly, for example:
# OIDC_PROVIDERS=keycloak,google
# OIDC_GOOGLE_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# OIDC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC_GOOGLE_LABEL=Google

# ── Keycloak container (docker compose --profile identity up) ──────────────
# KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
# KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin
# KEYCLOAK_PORT=8080
